 in Montreal, Quebec, Canada. It is located on the Quai King-Edward in the Old Port of Montreal. Established in 2000 and originally known as the iSci Centre, the museum changed its name to the Montreal Science Centre in 2002.","wpu":"Montreal Science Centre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Montreal_science_centre.jpg/960px-Montreal_science_centre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Place Jacques-Cartier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.507889,"lo":-73.553,"d":397,"ds":"Place Jacques-Cartier (French pronunciation: [plas ʒak kaʁtje], Jacques Cartier Square) is a square located in Old Montreal, Quebec, Canada. It is an entrance to the Old Port of Montreal.","wpu":"Place Jacques-Cartier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Place_Jacques-Cartier_115.jpg/960px-Place_Jacques-Cartier_115.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pointe-à-Callière Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.5026,"lo":-73.5542,"d":422,"ds":"Pointe-à-Callière Museum is a museum of archaeology and history in Old Montreal, Quebec, Canada. It was founded in 1992 as part of celebrations to mark Montreal's 350th birthday.","wpu":"Pointe-à-Callière Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pointe-a-Calliere_02.jpg/960px-Pointe-a-Calliere_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bonsecours Market","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.508889,"lo":-73.551389,"d":445,"ds":"Bonsecours Market (French: Marché Bonsecours) is a two-story domed public market located in Montreal, Quebec, Canada at 350 Rue Saint-Paul in Old Montreal. For more than 100 years, it was the main public market in the Montreal area. It also briefly accommodated the Parliament of United Canada for one session in 1849.","wpu":"Bonsecours Market","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/March%C3%A9_Bonsecours_in_Old_Montreal.jpg/960px-March%C3%A9_Bonsecours_in_Old_Montreal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Notre-Dame Basilica (Montreal)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.504444,"lo":-73.556111,"d":480,"ds":"The Basilica of Our Lady of Montreal, also known as Notre-Dame Basilica of Montreal (French: Basilique Notre-Dame de Montréal, lit. 'Basilica of Our Lady of Montreal'), is a minor basilica of the Catholic Church in the historic Old Montreal district of Montreal in Quebec, Canada. It is located at 110 Notre-Dame Street West, on the corner of Saint Sulpice Street.","wpu":"Notre-Dame Basilica (Montreal)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Bas%C3%ADlica_de_Notre-Dame%2C_Montreal%2C_Canad%C3%A1%2C_2017-08-11%2C_DD_26-28_HDR.jpg/960px-Bas%C3%ADlica_de_Notre-Dame%2C_Montreal%2C_Canad%C3%A1%2C_2017-08-11%2C_DD_26-28_HDR.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hommage à Marguerite Bourgeoys","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.50729,"lo":-73.55525,"d":481,"ds":"Hommage à Marguerite Bourgeoys is an outdoor 1988 sculpture and memorial depicting the founder of the Congregation of Notre Dame of Montreal of the same name by Jules Lasalle, installed in Place Marguerite-Bourgeoys, at 85 Notre-Dame Street East, in Montreal.","wpu":"Hommage à Marguerite Bourgeoys","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Marguerite_Bourgeoys_par_Jules_Lasalle_02.jpg/960px-Marguerite_Bourgeoys_par_Jules_Lasalle_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pont de la Concorde (Montreal)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.504167,"lo":-73.543889,"d":485,"ds":"The Pont De la Concorde (English: Concorde Bridge) carries Avenue Pierre-Dupuy across the St. Lawrence River between Cité du Havre, Montreal, and Parc Jean-Drapeau on Saint Helen's Island. Most of its traffic is from motorists driving to the Montreal Casino on Île Notre-Dame and continuing on Pierre Dupuy Avenue across Pont des Îles.","wpu":"Pont de la Concorde (Montreal)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Pont_de_la_Concorde_2023b.jpg/960px-Pont_de_la_Concorde_2023b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Montreal City Hall","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.5086,"lo":-73.5539,"d":502,"ds":"The five-story Montreal City Hall is the seat of local government in Montreal, Quebec, Canada. It was designed by architects Henri-Maurice Perrault and Alexander Cowper Hutchison, and built between 1872 and '78 in the Second Empire style. It is located in Old Montreal, between Place Jacques-Cartier and the Champ de Mars, at 275 Notre-Dame Street East.","wpu":"Montreal City Hall","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Montreal_City_Hall_2026.JPG/960px-Montreal_City_Hall_2026.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dieppe Park (Montreal)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.5057,"lo":-73.5434,"d":520,"ds":"Dieppe Park (French: Parc de Dieppe) is a park in Montreal, Quebec, Canada. It is at the eastern tip of Cité du Havre, in the borough of Ville-Marie. Between 1992 and 2017, it was known as Parc de la Cité-du-Havre.","wpu":"Dieppe Park (Montreal)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/St._Lawrence_Seaway_at_Montreal_Old_Port%2C_Quebec_Province%2C_Canada_-_panoramio_%287%29.jpg/960px-St._Lawrence_Seaway_at_Montreal_Old_Port%2C_Quebec_Province%2C_Canada_-_panoramio_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Notre-Dame-de-Bon-Secours Chapel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.51,"lo":-73.551111,"d":562,"ds":"The Notre-Dame-de-Bon-Secours Chapel (chapelle Notre-Dame-de-Bon-Secours, \"Our Lady of Good Help\") is a church in the district of Old Montreal in Montreal, Quebec. One of the oldest churches in Montreal, it was built in 1771 over the ruins of an earlier chapel.","wpu":"Notre-Dame-de-Bon-Secours Chapel","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Notre-Dame-de-Bon-Secours_Chapel_in_Old_Montreal.jpg/960px-Notre-Dame-de-Bon-Secours_Chapel_in_Old_Montreal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Place d'Armes","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.504722,"lo":-73.557222,"d":563,"ds":"Place d'Armes (French pronunciation: [plas daʁm]) is a square of the Old Montreal quarter of Montreal, Quebec, Canada. It is anchored by the Maisonneuve Monument, erected in memory of Paul de Chomedey, founder of Montreal. Buildings that surround it include Notre-Dame Basilica, Saint-Sulpice Seminary, New York Life Building, Aldred Building, Bank of Montreal head office and 500 Place D'Armes.","wpu":"Place d'Armes","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/J38002_ArtVille_20120407-134630_PlaceDArmes.jpg/960px-J38002_ArtVille_20120407-134630_PlaceDArmes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Champ de Mars (Montreal)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.508889,"lo":-73.555278,"d":596,"ds":"The Champ de Mars is a public park in Old Montreal quarter of Montreal, Quebec, Canada. Formerly a military parade ground, the park had previously been traversed by Montreal's fortifications, which were demolished at the beginning of the 19th century. The adjacent Montreal City Hall (1878) and the old courthouse (1856) were later built inside the line of the fortifications.","wpu":"Champ de Mars (Montreal)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Champ_de_mars_Montreal.JPG/960px-Champ_de_mars_Montreal.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"John Young Monument","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.500134,"lo":-73.553456,"d":604,"ds":"The John Young Monument (French: Monument à John Young) is a monument of Canadian politician John Young by sculptor Louis-Philippe Hébert. It is located at the Old Port of Montreal, where Young was the first Chairman of the Port Commission.","wpu":"John Young Monument","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Monument_John_Young.JPG/960px-Monument_John_Young.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pioneers' Obelisk (Montreal)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.500837,"lo":-73.555642,"d":638,"ds":"The Pioneers' Monument Obelisk is a monument in Old Montreal.","wpu":"Pioneers' Obelisk (Montreal)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pioneers%27_Obelisk%2C_Montreal.jpg/960px-Pioneers%27_Obelisk%2C_Montreal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Robillard Block","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.5075,"lo":-73.5591,"d":761,"ds":"The Robillard Building (French: Édifice Robillard) once located at 974, boulevard Saint Laurent (974, Saint Laurent Boulevard), was a landmark building in Montreal, Quebec, Canada, situated in Montreal's Chinatown on the corner of rue Viger (Viger Street) and boulevard Saint Laurent (Saint Laurent Boulevard). On 17 November 2016, the building was destroyed by fire.","wpu":"Robillard Block","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/2014-11-08_%C3%89difice_Robillard_Montr%C3%A9al_Chinatown_1.jpg/960px-2014-11-08_%C3%89difice_Robillard_Montr%C3%A9al_Chinatown_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Montreal (French: Montréal) is the largest city in the province of Quebec, the second-largest in Canada, and the eighth-largest in North America. Founded in 1642 as Ville-Marie, or \"City of Mary\", it now takes its name from Mount Royal, the triple-peaked mountain around which the early settlement was built. The city is centred on the Island of Montreal and several much smaller, peripheral islands, the largest of which is Île Bizard.","wpu":"Montreal","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Montreal%2C_Quebec_skyline.jpg/960px-Montreal%2C_Quebec_skyline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}